# Best stream-K win candidates, hand-picked from tier1-conv-streamk-mi350-configs
# for MI350 (CDNA4, gfx950, num_cu = 256).
#
# Selection: shapes where stream-K should clearly beat both data-parallel and
# split-K. Using the tuner's efficient tile, tile(d) = min(128, next_pow2(d)),
#   grid = ceil(gemmM/tile(gemmM)) * ceil(gemmN/tile(gemmN))
# each row satisfies ALL of:
#   (1) single ragged round: ceil(grid/num_cu) == 2, with the 2nd round only
#       ~12-33% full (grid only slightly > num_cu=256) -> under data-parallel
#       most CUs sit idle for much of the kernel. imbalance =
#       ceil(grid/num_cu)*num_cu/grid >= 1.50 (a >=1.50x data-parallel->balanced
#       ceiling).
#   (2) compute-bound: arithmetic intensity high (>=200 FLOP/byte here) and
#       gemmK large (>=256, splittable), so reclaimed idle CUs become real
#       matrix-core throughput.
#   (3) stream-K beats split-K: the imbalance is a small tail, so stream-K
#       re-splits only the few remainder tiles (few atomics), whereas split-K
#       would split every tile's K (broad atomic + output-prefill overhead) to
#       fix the same tail.
# Eligible dtypes on gfx950 (fast atomic_add): f16, f32.
#
# Trailing comment columns: imbalance | grid | gemmM | gemmN | gemmK | AI | GFLOP
#
# grid=288 cluster (imbalance 1.78, 2nd round ~12% full):
convfp16 -F 1 -f GNC01 -I NGC01 -O NGC01 -n 1 -c 512 -H 96 -W 96 -k 512 -y 3 -x 3 -p 1 -q 1 -u 1 -v 1 -l 1 -j 1 -g 1     # 1.78 | 288 | 512 |  9216 |  4608 | 1843 | 43.5
convfp16 -F 1 -f GNC01 -I NGC01 -O NGC01 -n 1 -c 224 -H 192 -W 192 -k 96 -y 3 -x 3 -p 1 -q 1 -u 1 -v 1 -l 1 -j 1 -g 1    # 1.78 | 288 |  96 | 36864 |  2016 |  595 | 14.3
